From 3af823e0562d1dd39f95110d066aee717a62f2eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "open-swe[bot]" <open-swe@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 08:09:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] fix(fs): accept-and-discard writes to FILE_DATA_FUNC
 files instead of erroring
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Refusing the write is not viable. Measured 2026-08-06: the Smart Card Shell's
SmartCardHSM.js aborts initialisation with 'GPError ... Unexpected SW1/SW2=6581'
the moment a write to one of these EFs is refused, so hsm-init-hardened.js (and
any scsh-driven provisioning) cannot complete. OpenSC happens to IGNORE the same
failure, which is why this only surfaced under scsh.

The memory-safety problem is still solved by not writing — ->data is a generator
function pointer, not storage. Only the reported status changes. The content is
regenerated from live card state on every read, so discarding the host's copy
loses nothing structural; the one visible effect is that a host-supplied label
does not stick, because parse_token_info() hardcodes it.

This resolves the semantic question deferred on polhenarejos/pico-keys-sdk#32,
now with hardware evidence for which behaviour is correct.
---
 src/fs/flash.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/fs/flash.c b/src/fs/flash.c
index 0075bce..5f88a96 100644
--- a/src/fs/flash.c
+++ b/src/fs/flash.c
@@ -224,7 +224,18 @@ static int flash_write_data_to_file_internal(file_t *file, const_byte_array_t da
      * hard_assert() and killing core0 while it held the multicore lockout with
      * interrupts disabled. The card enumerated but never answered again. */
     if ((file_get_type(file) & FILE_DATA_FUNC) == FILE_DATA_FUNC) {
-        return PICOKEYS_ERR_BLOCKED;
+        /* Accept and discard rather than error. The memory-safety problem is solved by not
+         * writing (->data is a function pointer, not storage); the remaining question is what
+         * to report. Returning an error is NOT viable: measured 2026-08-06, the Smart Card
+         * Shell's SmartCardHSM.js aborts initialisation with
+         * "GPError ... Unexpected SW1/SW2=6581" the moment a write to one of these EFs is
+         * refused, so hardened provisioning becomes impossible. OpenSC happens to ignore the
+         * same failure, which is why it only showed up under scsh.
+         *
+         * The content is regenerated on every read from live card state, so discarding the
+         * host's copy loses nothing structural — the one visible effect is that a host-supplied
+         * label does not stick, because parse_token_info() hardcodes it. */
+        return PICOKEYS_OK;
     }
 
     len = (uint32_t)data.len;
-- 
2.53.0

